4. Reference Scope and Responsibility

Use of STOCRS is entirely at the user's own risk.

STOCRS is a bounded reference implementation and research model for deterministic structural computation under the declared reference cases.

The current governing relation is:

same complete compatible structure + same frozen rules -> same supported structural result

The current bounded structural model includes:

incomplete structure -> unresolved

multi-value claim conflict -> conflicted node

structure-incompatible claim -> conflicted node

required dependency not resolved -> dependent node unresolved

claim multiplicity != structural authority

corrected compatible evidence -> resolution may proceed again

The current reference implementation demonstrates, within its declared cases, that systems evaluating the same complete compatible structure under the same frozen deterministic rules can produce the same supported structural result even when fragment arrival order and local clock values differ.

The current reference implementation also demonstrates explicit incompleteness, bounded conflict handling, deterministic result reproduction, frozen file-identity verification, and replay-oriented validation within the declared reference cases.

The reference implementation does not guarantee universal order independence, universal time independence, automatic convergence of arbitrary distributed protocols, automatic completion from bounded sharing, communication-free operation, coordination-free operation, consensus, factual truth of inputs, network reliability, security, system-level fault tolerance, regulatory compliance, production readiness, or fitness for financial, medical, safety-critical, or other high-risk use.

Time, timestamps, transport history, arrival order, and claim multiplicity are not treated as substitutes for declared structure and frozen deterministic rules as computational authority in the declared reference cases.

These statements do not imply that clocks, timestamps, sequencing, communication, networking, persistence, retries, scheduling, synchronization, coordination, consensus, or other operational mechanisms are universally unnecessary.


